A little belated because I've been more focused on a community project, but my answer was no, I don't need to make 16 different constructors.
The answer is a java builder pattern. I will be referring to this link to learn about them: https://www.baeldung.com/java-builder-pattern
That page brought up more questions than answers. Stackoverflow has a section saying "The builder pattern is a good choice when designing classes whose
constructors or static factories would have more than a handful of parameters," which matches my case exactly. It explains the telescoping constructor pattern
as having a problem that once the constructors are 4 or 5 parameters long, it becomes difficult to remember the parameter order. This is definitely true, and I'm
gonna continue being patient even though I don't see it mentioning the 2^n (n is number of arguments, testing the presence or absence) problem.
It seems like the simplicity comes from the fact that you're assigning values to a different object, then passing that object to the currently telescoped constructor.
Instead of calling Pizza pizza = new Pizza(true, true, false, true), you assign those values to a Builder object and pass the builder object to the Pizza constructor.
The pizza constructor would no longer accept a ton of arguments, just the one builder argument. Inside the Pizza constructor, the pizza's private parameters are set
to the builder's values. But wouldn't this just mean I have the same problem in a new place? This also confuses me because of the differences in arguments. I'm worried
about how it will work with non-booleans. Maybe the booleans are the test for the presence or absence of an argument?

This article might be more helpful, it actually mentions the presence of optional parameters
https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/builder-design-pattern-in-java

1. Create a static nested class (builder class) and copy all the arguments from the outer class (Assignment) into the Builder class. The builder class should be named
AssignmentBuilder.
2. The java Builder class should have a public constructor with all the required attributes as parameters. This is where I'm stuck. I think this means the non-optional
parameters, so all the parameters that aren't owner, priority, points, and due date.
3. The Java builder class should have methods that set the optional parameters and return the same Builder object after setting the optional attribute.
4. Provide a build() method in the builder class that will return the Object needed by client program (Assignment). For this, a private constructor is needed in the class
with Builder class as argument.
So, is the builder called when an assignment is needed, then separate logic inserts the chosen optional parameters into the Assignment object created by the Builder?
